# reading_translation = Unferth spoke, the son of Ecglaf, who sat at the feet of the lord of the Scyldings, he unbuckled a battle-rune—to him Beowulf's venture, the brave sea-farer's, was a great resentment, because he did not grant that any other man ever might obtain more glories in the world under the heavens than he himself—: 'Are you the Beowulf, the one who contended with Breca, on the wide sea competed in swimming, where you two for pride tested the waters and for foolish boasting ventured your lives in deep water?'
# literal_translation = Unferth spoke, Ecglaf's son, who at feet sat of-lord of-Scyldings, unbuckled battle-rune-- was to-him of-Beowulf journey, of-brave sea-farer, great resentment, because he not granted, that any other man ever of-glories than more of-middle-earth might-obtain under heavens than he himself--: 'Are you the Beowulf, the one who with Breca fought, on wide sea about swimming contended, where you-two for pride waters tested and for foolish-boasting in deep water lives risked?'
