# sent_id = beowulf.00148
# text = Hē þæt ful geþeah, wælrēow wiga æt Wealhþeon, ond þā gyddode gūþe gefȳsed; Bēowulf maþelode, beam Ecgþēowes: 'Ic þæt hogode, þā ic on holm gestāh, sǣbāt gesæt mid mīnra secga gedriht, þæt ic ānunga ēowra lēoda willan geworhte, oþðe on wæl crunge fēondgrāpum fæst.
# ref = 628-636
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = He, the slaughter-fierce warrior, accepted that cup at Wealhtheow's side, and then, stirred to battle, he recited a poem; Beowulf, son of Ecgtheow, spoke: 'I had intended, when I climbed onto the sea, sat in the sea-boat with the company of my men, that I would either altogether accomplish the will of your people, or fall in slaughter, fast in the grip of foes.
# literal_translation = He that cup accepted, slaughter-fierce warrior at Wealhtheow, and then chanted, to-battle urged; Beowulf spoke, son of-Ecgtheow: 'I that intended, when I on sea stepped, sea-boat sat with of-my men company, that I altogether of-your people will would-work, or in slaughter should-fall, foe-grips fast.
