# sent_id = beowulf.00237
# text = Ðā wæs swīgra secg, sunu Ec[g]lāfes, on gylpsprǣce gūðgeweorca, siþðan æþelingas eorles cræfte ofer hēanne hrōf hand scēawedon, fēondes fingras; foran ǣghwylc wæs, stī[r]a nægla gehwylc stȳle gelīcost, hǣþenes handsporu hilderinces egl[u] unhēoru; ǣghwylc gecwæð, þæt him heardra nān hrīnan wolde īren ǣrgōd, þæt ðæs āhlǣcan blōdge beadufolme onberan wolde.
# ref = 980-990
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Then the man, the son of Ecglaf, was more silent in boasting of deeds of war, after the nobles had examined the strength of the earl, the hand over the high roof, the fingers of the enemy; each of the stout nails was most like steel, the heathen's hand-spans, the warrior's terrible, monstrous claws; each said that no hard, ancient-good iron would touch him, that it would carry off the blood-stained battle-palm of that champion.
# literal_translation = Then was more-silent man, son Ecglaf's, in boasting-speech of war-works, after nobles of-earl by-strength over high roof hand examined, enemy's fingers; in-front each was, of-stout nails each steel most-like, heathen's hand-spans of-warrior terrible monstrous; each said, that to-him of-hard none touch would iron ancient-good, that of-that champion blood-stained battle-palm carry-off would.
