# sent_id = beowulf.00272
# text = Hengest ðā gȳt wælfāgne winter wunode mid Finne [ea]l unhlitme; eard gemunde, þēah þe hē meahte on mere drīfan hringedstefnan,-- holm storme wēol, won wið winde, winter ȳpe belēac īsgebinde, oþ ðæt ōþer cōm gēar in geardas,-- swā nū gȳt dêð, þā ðe syngāles sēle bewitiað, wuldortorhtan weder.
# ref = 1127-1136
# source_version = perseus-ang1
# model = deepseek-chat
# reading_translation = Hengest still dwelt with Finn through the slaughter-stained winter, [all] without blame; he remembered his homeland, though he could drive the ring-prowed ship on the sea—the ocean surged with storm, contended against the wind, winter locked the waves in ice-bonds, until another year came into the courts—as even now it does, those that ever watch over the seasons, gloriously bright weather.
# literal_translation = Hengest then yet slaughter-stained winter dwelt with Finn all without-blame; homeland remembered, though he might on sea drive ring-prowed-ship—the sea with storm surged, contended against wind, winter waves locked ice-bond, until that other came year into courts—so now yet does, those that continually seasons observe, glory-bright weather.
